# Always use the User Story Solution Architect for user stories

Whenever the user asks to **create, write, draft, plan, refactor, or break down** a
user story, requirement, build spec, technical specification, or epic, you MUST
first read and follow the skill at
`.cursor/skills/user-story-architect/SKILL.md` (the "User Story Solution
Architect"), and author the output using its contract.

## Triggers (non-exhaustive)

- "create/write/draft a user story", "story for …", "US-…", "acceptance criteria"
- "break down this into stories", "epic", "decompose the spec"
- "refactor story", "bug fix story", "build spec", "implementation plan"

## Non-negotiables from the skill (hard blockers)

- **Concrete business persona** — never "user"/"business user"/"developer". Use the
  workspace role (e.g., Credentialing Specialist, PDM Specialist, Provider Data
  Admin (PDA) Specialist, Network Management QC Specialist, Ancillary Cred Specialist).
- **Given / When / Then in business language** (Pattern A) for behavioural ACs — no
  Apex class names, IP versions/step numbers, SOQL, or API field names inside the AC.
- **`## Technical Implementation (high-level)`** section after the ACs (all
  implementation detail lives here, not in the ACs).
- **Never hallucinate component names** — verify OmniScripts/DataRaptors/IPs/Apex
  against the codebase first, and scan `requirements/` for overlapping stories.
- Include the required sections in canonical order and an **Estimated Effort** table.

## If the MCP is available

If the `project-0-IBXQA-user-story-architect` MCP server is connected, you may use
it. If it is unavailable, fall back to the skill file above — do not skip the
Architect contract.
